-- title:
--   Existence of an admissible scale step
-- statement:
--   Fix a dimension $d$, real parameters $\vartheta$ and $R_0$, and an integer $L > 0$. An *admissible scale step* is a natural number $\Delta$ that is
--
--   - **even**;
--   - larger than the geometric constant $\dfrac{\sqrt{d}+1}{\sin(\vartheta/2)}$;
--   - larger than $R_0$;
--   - divisible by $L$.
--
--   The assertion is that such a $\Delta$ always exists, for every $d$, every $\vartheta$, every $R_0$ and every $L > 0$.
--
--   The four requirements are exactly the demands a renormalization scheme places on its scale ratio: evenness and divisibility by the number $L$ of available cone types so that the geometry of the towers of cubes is compatible with the cone bookkeeping, and the two lower bounds so that each tower at ratio $\Delta$ is sparsely populated and each first jump has room to occur.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** No hypothesis is placed on $\vartheta$ — in particular it is not assumed to lie in $(0,\pi/2]$. When $\sin(\vartheta/2) = 0$ the constant $(\sqrt d + 1)/\sin(\vartheta/2)$ is Lean's junk value $0$ for division by zero, and when $\sin(\vartheta/2) < 0$ the constant is negative; in either case the bound is weaker than intended but the statement remains literally true. The conclusion is `Nonempty` of the bundling structure, i.e. bare existence with no canonical choice.
